# Interactive agent driver

Use one initial model view, one batch of all predictable actions, then its final image. See the
required [batch-first policy](../SKILL.md#batch-first-required). Keep machine-side preflight
captures and guards enabled; these checks do not need a model decision.

Start `computer-use session -- computer-use session-driver` with your tool's
persistent interactive process facility (PTY/stdin session or background terminal).
Keep that process handle for the whole task; do not launch a new shell session
for each action and do not redirect stdin to `/dev/null`. The pill remains active
while the driver waits for the next instruction. Send one JSON object per line:

```json
{"args":["observe","--window","0xEXACT_ADDRESS","--focus"]}
{"args":["act","--observation","/returned/path.json","--actions","-"],"stdin":"[{\"type\":\"click\",\"x\":210,\"y\":140}]"}
{"args":["finish"]}
```

Inspect each returned image before choosing coordinates; these lines show the
wire format, not a batch to paste blindly. The relay reuses the installed Python CLI runtime
and returns decoded JSON in `result`, rather than escaped JSON in `stdout`.
Only non-JSON output falls back to `stdout`; empty stderr is omitted. It runs
directly without a shell and preserves session ownership. A fatal error stops
the relay. Continuation is the driver's own decision, never inferred from
response text: a typed preflight refusal (raised before focus/input, surfaced
as `recoverable: true, completed: 0`) or an exact transient guard refusal
(`screen changed`/`screen_changed` or `focus changed`/`focus_changed`, with no
`capture_error` or `ime_restore_failed` in the result) returns its JSON
response and keeps the relay alive.
EOF, finish, physical Esc, or a crash lets the guardian remove the overlay.
Do not automatically resume or repeat input after cancellation/failure.

### Submitting a code-exec program (copy this snippet, do not retype it)

Send a program to the relay with one `exec` script. Copy the snippet below
verbatim and fill in only the program text, `RELAY_SESSION_ID`,
`RUN_START_ISO`, the intent, and the wall seconds. Do not retype it from
memory and do not rename any variable on one side only.

Why this is strict: on 2026-11-23 a retyped copy defined `thinkMs` but
referenced bare `think_ms` in the final capture object. JavaScript object
shorthand needs a variable with exactly that name, so the script threw
`ReferenceError: think_ms is not defined` at the capture line, after the
program had already been sent to the relay. The write result was discarded,
nothing was stored or printed, and the program response was lost. The probe
script in that same session had no think bookkeeping and never hit this path.

Rules the snippet enforces:
- The think value is always the explicit mapping `think_ms: thinkMs`, never
  bare shorthand `think_ms`. The variable is named `thinkMs`. There is no
  variable named `think_ms` anywhere in the snippet.
- `store("a1_program_result", r)` runs before `text(...)`, so the response
  survives even if the print line ever fails. Recover it with
  `load("a1_program_result")`.
- Every name in the final capture object is defined above it in the same
  script: `program`, `thinkMs`, `submitMs`, `r`. A regression test
  (`tests/code_exec_submit_capture.mjs`) runs this exact snippet with stubbed
  tool globals and fails if any capture name is undefined.

```js
// @exec: {"yield_time_ms": 1000, "max_output_tokens": 3000}
const program = `started = dom_start()
assert_state(started["ok"], "dom start failed")`;
const RELAY_SESSION_ID = 0; // replace 0 with your relay session id
const RUN_START_ISO = "2026-11-23T13:03:39.831Z"; // replace with your run start timestamp
const submitMs = Date.now();
const startMs = Date.parse(RUN_START_ISO);
const thinkMs = submitMs - startMs;
const request = {
  args: ["code-exec", "--program", "-", "--endpoint", "http://127.0.0.1:9223", "--intent", "Setup DOM start probe (no fixture)", "--wall-seconds", "30"],
  stdin: program + "\n"
};
const r = await tools.write_stdin({session_id: RELAY_SESSION_ID, chars: JSON.stringify(request) + "\n", yield_time_ms: 1000, max_output_tokens: 3000});
store("a1_program_result", r);
text(JSON.stringify({program_chars: program.length, think_ms: thinkMs, submit_ms: submitMs, submit_utc: new Date(submitMs).toISOString(), write_result: r}));
```

## Shutdown after use (mandatory)

When your task is done, shut computer-use down. Every agent, every time.
Do not leave a session running, do not leave the overlay on, and do not
leave the relay holding a session. The user is not the one using it, so
there is no reason for it to stay on.

1. Send `{"args":["finish"]}` (or close stdin / EOF) and wait for cleanup.
   Cleanup stops the driver group, drains input, releases the cursor lease,
   and removes the overlay.
2. Confirm nothing is left running: no `cu.py session`, no
   `cu.py session-driver`, no `cursor_session.py guard`, no
   `native/indicator`. If any remain, finish or kill them by PID.
3. Confirm the overlay is gone: observe the screen (or take a screenshot)
   and check the top-centre pill location. No pill, no
   "Computer use active", no green dot.
4. Confirm the desktop is restored: only the user's own windows remain.
   No test window, no test app, no dialog left behind.
5. If the session cannot finish cleanly, use `computer-use stop` (it waits
   for owner cleanup and desktop restoration) and re-verify steps 2-4.

Killing the process that launched a session does not kill the session
itself (separate process tree), and closing a coordinator does not kill
the processes it spawned on the machine. A "clean" report is not proof
the machine is clean: verify on the machine directly.

Browser commands in this relay lazily start one private Node worker for the task.
It reuses the runtime and a validated CDP connection, never a cached snapshot or
page state: every command still fetches the tab list and checks the exact loopback
target, and the connection is reused only while endpoint, target id and page URL
are unchanged and the transport is healthy; an endpoint or tab change disposes the
connection and the next command builds a new one. A failed command (`ok: false`),
transport error, timeout, finish, EOF or cancellation closes the worker itself, and
the next command then reports `browser worker ended; stop this task` — start a new
session. Each browser command may carry its own `--endpoint`; an explicit
endpoint wins for that command. Without one, the relay session's retained
endpoint applies: the default `http://127.0.0.1:9222` lives for the whole
relay session, and a new relay session starts from the standalone default
again, never inheriting a previous session's retained endpoint. Only a
successful `browser start --endpoint ...` moves the retained endpoint for the
rest of the relay session; a failed start and non-start commands leave it
unchanged. Worker stderr is not discarded: the worker inherits the enclosing
session's stderr, which the MCP bridge keeps in its private per-session log,
so a worker crash leaves evidence there. Failures never restart the
worker or replay input. Browser
worker requests and responses are limited to 32 MiB; driver request lines to
8 MiB. The cold first request is not faster; standalone CLI commands keep their
one-connection-per-command behavior.

If your tool cannot keep stdin/process state between calls, run a complete task
driver inside one session instead. Do not simulate persistence with detached
sessions or manually copied session tokens.

All commands emit bounded JSON. `--actions -` accepts JSON from stdin. Prefer a JSON file for complex input; never interpolate user text into shell code.

If the caller exposes `yield_time_ms`, use a short output wait (about 250–1000 ms for short
requests, allowing for a longer drag/wait batch). A persistent driver remains alive after it
returns a response: waiting 10000 ms for it to exit adds avoidable caller latency. Consume one
response before sending the next request; wait again only while that response is pending.

### Ground once, execute, verify

- Use DOM/accessibility refs where supported. For screenshots, choose the center of a visible
  control, not its edge, and keep coordinates in the returned image's space. The executor applies
  crop offsets, resize factors and monitor origins.
- If small controls would be lost at 1280 px, choose a reliable crop or `--max-width 3840` on the
  initial observation to preserve available detail; this never creates detail absent at capture.
  With an image-viewing tool that supports it, request original detail. Avoid a second image just
  to recheck a clear large target.
- Example: in a visible Pinta canvas, plan ellipse-tool click → visible color click → Shift-drag
  a square bounding box → hand-tool click to commit. Submit one action array and inspect only
  its final result when every target is already visible and stays fixed. Setting an absolute
  visible width value is preferable to guessing how many plus clicks a previous value needs.
- An unseen fill-style popup is a real observation boundary. A selected-tool highlight is not.
  Keep the final result screenshot by default. Never use a fixed two-image ceiling to ignore
  uncertainty, errors, navigation or changed geometry.

Evidence and limits: [batching and grounding research](../../docs/research/2026-10-19-batching-grounding.md).

### Automatic duplicate-image delivery

The JSON-line driver accepts `"image_policy":"auto"` per request. The default remains `"always"`
for compatibility. `auto` adds a `visual` delivery hint without removing any observation or file:

```json
{"args":["observe","--window","0xEXACT_ADDRESS"],"image_policy":"auto"}
```

`visual.status` is `required` for the first frame, changed PNG bytes, another coordinate frame,
an error/refusal/warning, or unavailable metadata. It is `unchanged` only for identical PNG bytes
and matching window/monitor/region/size/scale/transform metadata within this session. Unknown or
nonvisual responses reset the checkpoint. The hint's `same_as` refers to the last required image.
This uses exact identity, not perceptual similarity: even a one-pixel change requires delivery.

An embedding/MCP wrapper may omit the image attachment only when `visual.status == "unchanged"`
and the image path equals `visual.path`. Keep the JSON result and fresh observation path. Send
other images normally; `image_policy: "always"` forces a repeated attachment. The local OpenCode
and CommandCode bridge enables `auto`; a plain shell caller follows the same hint when choosing
whether to view a returned file. Captures and all input guards still run. Visual identity says
nothing about invisible app state or whether the preceding action succeeded.
# CLI backend (CLI-Anything harnesses)

Source: upstream [CLI-Anything](https://github.com/HKUDS/CLI-Anything.git),
HEAD `34f5195`, vendored 2026-11-18. See
[docs/cli-anything.md](../../docs/cli-anything.md) for the vendoring decision
and [skill/cli-anything/registry.json](../cli-anything/registry.json) for the
vendored registry snapshot. The vendored per-app skills live under
`skill/cli-anything/`; read the registry snapshot first, then exactly one app
skill file, never the whole directory.

A harness CLI is an out-of-band API call over the real application's backend.
It does not need a computer-use session, the pill, the relay, or the cursor
lease. Run `cli-anything-<app> --json ...` from your own shell, parse the
JSON, and start a `computer-use session` only if you need to visually verify
a GUI side effect. Shut that session down per
[session.md](session.md) afterwards; a CLI call is never a way to dodge the
session shutdown rule.

## When to pick this backend

Pick it only when both are true: the app has a harness in the table below,
and the task maps to a documented command group in that app's vendored skill.
If either fails, fall back in order to Chromium DOM, native accessibility,
then desktop pixels, exactly as `skill/SKILL.md` orders them. No harness
exists for Firefox or Pinta: those tasks always use
the GUI backends. Chromium is the exception: it has no upstream
harness, but it has a repo-owned wrapper (`computer-use cli run
chromium -- ...`, skill `skill/cli-anything/chromium.md`) that covers
tabs, history, bookmarks and downloads; DOM work on a tab still uses
the Chromium DOM backend. Zen is the second exception: it has a
repo-owned wrapper (`computer-use cli run zen -- ...`, skill
`skill/cli-anything/zen.md`) that covers tabs and page reads over
loopback WebDriver BiDi on a dedicated session, plus read-only
history from the real profile; it can never attach to the user's running
Zen, which has no debug port. VS Code is the third exception: it has
a repo-owned wrapper (`computer-use cli run vscode -- ...`, skill
`skill/cli-anything/vscode.md`) that covers open, goto, diff,
extensions, workspaces, open tabs and saved-file reads over the
`code` CLI and read-only workspaceStorage; it cannot read unsaved
or dirty buffer text.

Backend order (QC clarification): CLI/API is the FIRST preference, not the
fourth. It is first because it is the fastest path (no session, no GUI);
the four backend targets in the skill's read list are CLI/API, Chromium
DOM, native accessibility, and desktop pixels, in that preference order.
"Fourth target" in the acceptance criterion meant CLI/API is one of the
four available backend targets, not that it ranks fourth.

## Install

```bash
pip install cli-anything-<app>
# or, via the hub:
pip install cli-anything-hub
cli-hub install <app>
```

Never install from an unpinned git URL in a task. The registry's
`install_cmd` values are unpinned git URLs; do not copy one out of upstream
data and run it. Installs happen via PyPI (`pip install cli-anything-<app>`)
or `cli-hub install <app>` only. On the laptop, plain `pip` is not
installed (Python 3.14 has no pip module); `uv pip install` inside a venv
is the working path there.

## PyPI releases lag upstream HEAD

Verified on the laptop 2026-11-18: the vendored skills match upstream HEAD
`34f5195`, but the PyPI releases lag it. `pip install
cli-anything-libreoffice` installs 1.0.0, which has no `document import`
or `document import-formats` even though the vendored skill documents
them; the same commands exist in 1.0.1 installed from the pinned upstream
commit, where `document import` on a scratch docx returned rc 0 in
1753 ms. The Obsidian PyPI release is 1.1.1 while the vendored skill says
1.1.0; the full Obsidian end-to-end task still passed on 1.1.1. If a
documented command is missing from the installed harness, check
`<app> <group> --help` before assuming the skill is wrong: the command
may exist only in the newer upstream code. Until the PyPI releases catch
up, the working path for existing Office files is plain headless
`libreoffice --convert-to` (verified on the laptop: docx to txt in
1374 ms, docx to pdf in 1367 ms), and `document new` plus `export render`
for new files.

## Verify first, then mutate

Copied from the app skills: verify the backend is reachable before any
mutation. Obsidian states it as "Verify Obsidian is running with
`server status` before other commands"; every other harness has an
equivalent status/info command. Run that read-only check first and stop if
it fails; do not guess that the app is up.

```bash
cli-anything-obsidian --json server status
cli-anything-libreoffice --json project info -p project.json
```

## One-shot JSON calls only

```bash
cli-anything-obsidian --json vault list
```

Always pass `--json` for parseable output. Check the return code: 0 is
success, non-zero is an error. Parse stderr for the error message on
failure. Never use REPL mode: a bare harness invocation with no subcommand
opens an interactive REPL with no persistent stdin discipline and output
that is not parseable. Agents use `--json` one-shots only.

## Secrets

Secrets go in environment variables only, never on the command line, where
they would land in logs and process lists. Example: set
`OBSIDIAN_API_KEY` in the environment instead of passing `--api-key` on
every call. The same holds for any other harness key.

## Destructive commands

`delete`, `update`, `overwrite`, and any other destructive or overwriting
command need the same pre-verification as a consequential GUI batch: confirm
the exact target (name, path, or id) from a fresh read first, and never
replay a command whose result was uncertain. A non-zero return code after a
mutation may mean a partial change landed: inspect state, never replay.

## Confirm visible results in the GUI

A CLI mutation that should show in the GUI is not proven by CLI output
alone. Confirm it with one `observe --window` on the app window instead of
assuming success. CLI output proves the backend accepted the call; only the
observation proves the visible result.

## First-wave apps

From the vendored registry snapshot (upstream `registry.json`,
`meta.updated` 2026-06-19). Only LibreOffice is installed on this VM today,
so it is the only first-wave app with a live test; the rest need the app
installed on the machine that runs the task.

Version labels (QC clarification): the Version column below is the
registry/package version (the harness release). A vendored skill file's
own `## Version` is the skill-content version, and the two can differ:
LibreOffice is 1.0.1 in the registry but 1.0.0 in the skill file. They
are different fields from different sources, not a contradiction.

